{
  "name": "paged",
  "integration": "forescout-eyeinspect",
  "description": "The hosts listing pages by check-point, not by position. Page one is exactly full at the requested limit of 100 and page two is short, which is the only way to reach a second page.\n\nPage two must be requested with id_min=10100 rather than offset=100. The API Guide documents id_min as \"Retrieve hosts from a minimum ID value onward. Useful for check-pointing data\", which walks the table by primary key instead of by position -- an offset walk silently skips a row every time the Command Center inserts a host behind the cursor, and a Command Center is writing that table continuously. offset is what page ONE sends, because there is no check-point yet, and it stays the fallback for a page that carries no numeric id to advance from.\n\nid_min reads as inclusive, so the check-point passed is the last id seen (10100) rather than one past it -- correct whether the Command Center treats it as >= or >. Page two therefore re-delivers host 10100 as its first row, and the walk must drop that duplicate locally: 102 assets, not 103. That is what proves the boundary handling, and it is why page two carries three rows.\n\nThe third route answers 500 so a walk that runs on would fail loudly rather than quietly looping. include_alerts is off so the per-host alerts call does not drown the paging assertions.",
  "kwargs": {
    "url": "$BASE",
    "username": "fixture-operator",
    "password": "fixture-password",
    "last_seen_days": "0",
    "include_alerts": "false",
    "include_cve_details": "false"
  },
